---
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import FeatureVisual from '../../components/FeatureVisual.astro';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StatusPill from '../../components/StatusPill.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Presentation Authoring"
  description="Author ordered presentation decks from editable frames in Varve Design. Source-build preview; delivery features are in development."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Presentations
      </nav>
      <StatusPill status="experimental" label="Source-build preview" />
      <h1 class="site-page-title">Presentation authoring in Design</h1>
      <p class="feature-intro">
        Build a slide sequence from ordinary editable frames on the Design Canvas. The deck owns its
        order; canvas position and layer order stay available for the rest of your design work.
      </p>

      <section class="feature-section presentation-visuals" aria-labelledby="presentation-visuals-title">
        <h2 id="presentation-visuals-title">A local deck from canvas to delivery</h2>
        <p>
          The same editable frames appear in the Slides navigator, on the canvas, in audience preview,
          and in exported files. Preview navigation does not change the document or reveal private notes.
        </p>
        <FeatureVisual scene="presentation-navigator" priority />
        <FeatureVisual scene="presentation-slide" />
        <FeatureVisual scene="presentation-layout-revision" />
        <FeatureVisual scene="presentation-preview" />
        <FeatureVisual scene="presentation-export" />
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>One canvas, an explicit slide order</h2>
        <p>
          Create a presentation with a 16:9, 4:3, vertical, or custom first slide, or assemble a deck
          from selected frames after reviewing their order. The Slides navigator supports titles,
          private speaker notes, sections, skipped drafts, multi-selection, and visible Move earlier,
          Move later, and Move to position controls. Removing a slide reference keeps its frame artwork.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each slide remains editable in the normal Design canvas and participates in the document's
          save and undo history. Multiple decks can reference the same artwork, and missing frame
          references remain listed with their notes so they can be repaired.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Layouts you can see, trace, and undo</h2>
        <p>
          Applying a layout records what it managed on each object. The Slides layout manager then
          reports every mapped object as Inherited, Locally changed, or Object removed, names the
          exact property that changed, and offers a reset for that one property or for the whole
          slide. Reset restores only the values the layout owns, so corner radii, gradients, fills,
          effects, text, and object identity are untouched. Detaching removes the tracking metadata
          only; the artwork on screen is unchanged, because nothing stays live between a layout
          source and a slide.
        </p>
        <p>
          This targets a specific failure other tools leave with users: Figma staff state that a
          slide's layout cannot be changed after creation, and forum threads describe people
          rebuilding imported slides by hand. Reapplying here is always previewed and confirmed, and
          it is one undo step.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>One Present command</h2>
        <p>
          Presenting a deck document plays the deck; presenting a document without one still plays
          the prototype. The same command routes the decision, so the menubar, the keyboard shortcut,
          and the command palette never disagree about what Present means. Deck commands are also
          reachable directly from the palette: present from the current slide, export the deck, or
          add selected frames.
        </p>
        <p>
          During playback the stage owns the keyboard: Space and Enter advance, the arrows and
          PageUp/PageDown move, Home and End jump, and Escape exits with focus returned to the control
          that opened the preview. Reaching either end stops and announces the boundary rather than
          wrapping silently, so a presenter can tell a finished deck from a dropped keypress.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section note">
        <h2>Current source-build preview</h2>
        <p>
          The current source build covers deck creation, ordered slide navigation with rendered
          thumbnails, private notes, sections, audience preview, and multipage PDF or PNG-sequence
          delivery for either the whole deck or selected slide references. This capability is not yet
          included in a published Varve release.
        </p>
        <p>
          PDF is a compressed raster screen document, one page per included slide. It preserves
          appearance, but its text and vectors are not editable or searchable and it has no tagged
          accessibility structure. Speaker notes stay private. PNG output is an ordered archive with
          numbered slide filenames. Skipped and scene-hidden slides are excluded; an included missing
          frame blocks export and remains repairable in Slides.
        </p>
        <p>
          Initial slide checks flag missing artwork, placeholder text, small type, transformed
          off-slide content, unavailable fonts or image assets, low contrast when solid foreground
          and background colors are known, and slides without alt descriptions. These checks are
          advisory except for missing included artwork, and contrast is skipped when the background
          is ambiguous. Geometry-only reusable layout sources are also in the source build: map stable
          roles, review reflow/fit/crop, then apply in one undoable step. Slide text, paint, effects,
          crop intent, overrides, and unmatched artwork stay intact. Seven original editable layout
          sources cover title/section, body, image/text, comparison, evidence, process, and conclusion;
          they live on a dedicated Design Canvas and use bundled typography with no external stock
          media. On top of that, per-object override reporting, single-property and whole-slide
          geometry reset, and appearance-preserving detach are in the source build. Preview and the
          delivered PNG are checked against each other in the test suite — the same slide rasterizes
          identically in both, at the deck's declared pixel size. Detailed readability analysis and
          formatting inheritance remain in development.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Change the format without losing the work</h2>
        <p>
          Pick 16:9, 4:3, vertical, or a custom size and the deck shows a before-and-after review
          first: how many slides are affected, what happens to their content, and every warning.
          <strong>Fit inside</strong> and <strong>Fill and crop</strong> keep proportions;
          <strong>Stretch</strong> is the only mode that changes them, is labelled as such, and is
          never applied by accident. The conversion is one undo step, frames keep their canvas
          position, and artwork outside the deck is untouched.
        </p>
        <p>
          A theme links each role — title, subtitle, body, caption, accent, background — to a real
          document colour variable. Editing that variable in the Variables panel recolours every
          slide that still follows it, so a brand change is one edit rather than a walk through the
          deck. Objects you deliberately recoloured are left alone when you switch, and
          <em>Detach theme</em> bakes the colour that is on screen so the object stops following the
          brand without changing how it looks.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Built around real workflow failures</h2>
        <p>
          Existing-layout rebuilding, masks disappearing in slide export, and export actions that
          become unavailable are documented as regression scenarios. The roadmap treats layout
          reapplication as a preview-and-confirm operation, requires output checks against the editor,
          and keeps failures actionable instead of silently omitting content.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read the <a href={sitePath('/docs/presentations')}>presentation authoring guide</a> for
          current behavior and the documented delivery boundaries.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="feature-cta">
        <h2>Follow the source-build preview</h2>
        <p>This presentation workflow is not included in a published Varve release yet.</p>
        <Button href={sitePath('/docs/presentations')}>Read the presentation guide</Button>
      </div>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: var(--type-interface-readout-size); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .feature-page h1 { font-family: var(--font-editorial); margin: 1rem 0; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-intro { font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 3rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .feature-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .presentation-visuals { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .presentation-visuals :global(.feature-visual) { margin-block: 1.5rem 2.5rem; }
  .feature-section h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-section p { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .note { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary); padding-left: 1rem; }
  .feature-cta { padding: 1.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-card); background: var(--surface-card); }
  .feature-cta h2 { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-cta p { color: var(--text-secondary); }
</style>
